nmap TCP SYN ping method (Identify the host is alive)
By default, nmap will send a tcp SYN packets to the port 80 that is running on the target system, 
if the host is closed, it will response a TCP RST packet, and in the opposite case the host responses SYN+ACK packet
indicating that the connection is stablished, and afterwards, nmap will finish the connection with the RST packet. 
Or if the port 80 is not running on the target, the host will response as a RST packet,
whatever nmap receives SYN+ACK packets or RST packets, that all mean the host is Online.

nmap -sn -PS<PORT> <target>
nmap -sn -PS3389 <target> //Windows RDP
or specify nmap -sn -PS3389,80,443 <target>


nmap TCP ACK ping method
Sending a TCP ACK packet to the default port 80, if the host is up, will response an RST packet,
because according to the 3-ways-handshake our attack machine has not stablished a TCP connection with
the target system, and by default from the protocol, the target system will response a RST to define 
this ACK does not valid or does no exits(the connection). In the case of the host is offline, our attack
machine will no receive any response.
nmap -sn -PA<PORT> <target>

